"""Render a reviewer-facing report directly from committed calculated evidence."""
import json
from pathlib import Path
R=Path(__file__).parent
r=json.loads((R/'data/robustness.json').read_text());a=json.loads((R/'data/advanced-examples.json').read_text())
money=lambda x:f'${x:,.2f}'
i=r['experiment_interval_cents'];b=a['mrr_bridge']
rows=''.join(f'<tr><td>{x["effect_multiplier"]}×</td><td>{x["margin"]:.0%}</td><td>{x["cost_multiplier"]}×</td><td>{money(x["net_cash_usd"]["recovery"])}</td><td>{money(x["net_cash_usd"]["activation"])}</td><td>{money(x["net_cash_usd"]["renewal"])}</td><td>{x["candidate"].title()}</td></tr>' for x in r['sensitivity']['rows'])
bridge=''.join(f'<tr><th scope="row">{name.title()}</th><td>{money(b[name]/100)}</td></tr>' for name in ['opening','new','reactivation','expansion','contraction','churn','closing','difference'])
html=f'''<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><title>Decision evidence review | Revenue & Retention</title><link rel="stylesheet" href="/projects/revenue-retention/style.css"></head><body><a class="skip" href="#main">Skip to review</a><header><a href="/projects/revenue-retention">← Revenue & Retention</a><span>Methods / acceptance evidence</span></header><main id="main"><section class="hero"><p class="eyebrow">DATA / BUSINESS / PRODUCT</p><h1>Challenge the result.<br><em>Before acting on it.</em></h1><p class="lede">A review of financial adjustments, subscription changes and uncertainty. Every figure below is generated from executable evidence.</p><div class="disclosure"><b>Separate synthetic acceptance cases.</b> These are designed tests, not the dashboard company's transactions or a real experiment. No realized business value is claimed.</div></section><section><p class="eyebrow">01 / EXPERIMENT DECISION</p><h2>A signal is not permission to launch.</h2><div class="recommendation"><h3>Hold rollout</h3><p>Observed synthetic difference: {money(i['effect']/100)} per assigned account. Conservative 95% effect interval: {money(i['lower']/100)} to {money(i['upper']/100)}.</p></div><p>The exact sharp-null test returns p = {a['experiment_signal']['p_value']:.6f} (2 of 924 allocations). The separate bounded average-effect interval still includes harm. It uses prespecified $0–$300 bounds on both potential outcomes, complete random assignment and complete follow-up. It is intentionally conservative; these assumptions must be defensible before applying it to real data.</p><p>Missing outcomes stop the calculation. Statistical REVIEW never authorizes rollout. No power or real-world effect is inferred from this small fixture.</p><a href="/projects/revenue-retention/robustness-review.md">Read the uncertainty method and assumptions →</a></section><section><p class="eyebrow">02 / FINANCIAL CONTROL</p><h2>One credit. One refund. No double counting.</h2><div class="two"><article class="panel"><h3>Credit issued, refund pending</h3><p>$100 collected − $20 credit leaves $80 net billed, $100 cash and a $20 customer credit. Receivable: $0.</p></article><article class="panel"><h3>Refund completed</h3><p>$80 net billed, $80 net cash, $0 receivable and $0 customer credit. Neither adjustment changes contracted MRR.</p></article></div><p>These controls cover one invoice, reject duplicate or excessive adjustments and exclude events after cutoff. Proration, credit reversals, tax and accounting recognition remain outside scope.</p></section><section><p class="eyebrow">03 / SUBSCRIPTION ECONOMICS</p><h2>Explain every dollar of change.</h2><div class="panel table-scroll"><table><caption>Independent six-account example · USD</caption><thead><tr><th>Component</th><th>Value</th></tr></thead><tbody>{bridge}</tbody></table></div><p>Opening + new + reactivation + expansion − contraction − churn = closing. Gross retention: {b['grr']:.2%}; net retention: {b['nrr']:.2%}. Both exclude new and reactivated accounts. Missing snapshots stop analysis; a zero opening base returns unavailable retention.</p></section><section><p class="eyebrow">04 / BUSINESS SENSITIVITY</p><h2>Does the candidate survive changed assumptions?</h2><p>$1,000 budget, 20 staff hours, one pilot. Values are modeled 90-day net cash. Candidates must be affordable and positive. Cost multipliers affect spend; pilot workloads remain fixed.</p><div class="panel table-scroll"><table><caption>27 joint scenarios · unvalidated planning assumptions, not probability estimates</caption><thead><tr><th>Effect</th><th>Margin</th><th>Cost</th><th>Recovery</th><th>Activation</th><th>Renewal</th><th>Candidate</th></tr></thead><tbody>{rows}</tbody></table></div><p>Recovery uses sunk prior delivery costs; margin affects retention alternatives. Never treat the number of winning scenarios as a probability of success. Full collection and immediate retention effects are assumptions.</p></section><section><p class="eyebrow">05 / REVIEWER HANDOFF</p><h2>Reproduce. Inspect. Disagree.</h2><div class="downloads"><a href="/projects/revenue-retention/data/robustness.json">Robustness results JSON ↗</a><a href="/projects/revenue-retention/data/advanced-examples.json">MRR and exact-test results ↗</a><a href="/projects/revenue-retention/README.md">Commands, contracts and scope ↗</a><a href="https://github.com/harshit-malik-nyu/portfolio/tree/main/projects/revenue-retention">Implementation and acceptance tests ↗</a></div><p>Forty Python and seven JavaScript checks cover selected defects. Source import reproduces the primary results with file hashes. This is authored verification, not independent certification or proof of production-scale performance.</p></section></main><footer><a href="/projects/revenue-retention">Return to interactive workspace</a></footer></body></html>'''
(R/'review.html').write_text(html+'\n')
